<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.12.1: Using Coefficients and Constant Terms to Identify Graphs of Linear Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01945</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.12.1: Using Coefficients and Constant Terms to Identify Graphs of Linear Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>76a8b0e8-f9bd-4557-8cbc-c14baedbe7a8</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00001"><title>Warm Up</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Graphs A, B, and C represent three linear equations:</para>
      <para id="para-00002"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00003"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00004"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00005">Use the following graph to answer questions 1 – 3.</para>
      <para id="para-00006">
          <media alt="A coordinate plane with three graphed lines. The red line represents graph A. The green line represents graph B. The blue line represents line C.">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_14_02_01.svg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>

        <exercise id="a31de486-8285-47f6-a609-39ddfc463ace" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00007">Which graph corresponds to equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00008">The graph representing <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> has a slope of 2 and intersects the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis at 4, so it is Graph B.</para>
           
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="e11d53d3-0467-49e8-b238-d3076d8bb3b4" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00010">Which graph corresponds to equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00011">The graph representing <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> has a slope of -1 and intersects the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis at 3, so it is Graph A.</para>
          
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_701f4be7-b80f-4b52-bf9f-373ce76324db" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00013">Which graph corresponds to equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00014">The graph representing <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> has a slope of 3 and intersects the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis at -2, so it is Graph C.</para>
           
          </solution>
        </exercise>

      <note id="note-00001">
        <title>Building Character: Proactivity</title>
              <media alt="A person with long dark hair raises their hand while sitting at a desk with papers. Behind them are a clock, a bulletin board, a shelf with boxes, and a globe.">
              <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_14_02_02.png" width="200"/>
            </media>
          
          
            <para id="para-00017">Proactive people don’t accept the world as it is or wait for direction from others to initiate change. Instead, they take matters into their own hands, acting to make the world better for themselves and others. They speak truth to power, take charge to solve problems, and champion new ideas.</para>
            <para id="para-00018">Think about your current sense of proactivity. Are the following statements true for you??</para>
            <list id="list-00001">
              <item>
          I enjoy being a champion for ideas, even against opposition.
       </item>
              <item>
          I like to be the one to take initiative.
       </item>
            </list>
            <para id="para-00019">Don’t worry if none of these statements are true for you. Developing this trait takes time. Your first step starts today!</para>
          
        
      </note>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>
